;;; Two `Fact` arguments in one hypothesis slot.
;;;
;;; `mixed-type-hypothesis.ein` puts two names in the same slot of two
;;; candidates. This is the *other* half of the same ordering question, a
;;; find of the engine fuzzer: two candidates whose argument is a nested
;;; fact, compared element-wise by content. Values are totally ordered (a name
;;; before a fact, and two facts by their relation's name, then their
;;; arguments), so the pair sorts like any other, and R is four states, every
;;; commitment of the two (`ein solve FILE`).
;;;
;;; **It needs no mixed slot types.** One rule whose hypothesis is a stored
;;; negation, `(open (not …))`, is enough — its argument is then a nested fact,
;;; and two of them are candidates. That is an ordinary puzzle idiom, so the
;;; case is wider than its first fixture suggested. Blind generation cannot
;;; get here: its candidates' arguments are the program's names, never a
;;; fact, so a rule that concludes `(open F)` is necessary.
;;;
;;;

(is-a o1 T)
(is-a o2 T)

(rule hyp-0 ()
  :match  (is-a ?v0 T)
  :assert (open (not (r0 ?v0 o2))) :why "guess that r0 does not hold of {?v0} and o2")
